import { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";
import { randomUUID } from "node:crypto";

const db = new DatabaseSync("board.sqlite");
const B = "720aa8c1-d259-4e49-b777-c58965efabd1";
const col = (n) => db.prepare("SELECT id FROM columns WHERE boardId = ? AND name = ?").get(B, n).id;
const DONE = col("Done — awaiting deploy");
const NA = col("Needs Attention");
const AW = "\n\nSTATUS: complete and verified locally. NOT deployed.";

const MOVES = [
  ["%Prompt / build log inspector%", `Built at /admin/builds — list, detail, and automatic comparison against the project's most recent OTHER successful build, with each fallback labelled as the weaker signal it is.

The N+1 it had to solve is genuinely awkward: build.rated events store their buildId inside Event.props JSON with NO foreign key, so it cannot be a Prisma relation. One raw query with props->>'buildId' IN (...) for the whole page instead of a lookup per row.

The error-family filter cannot be pushed into SQL — the rules are JS regexes and \\b means something else in Postgres ARE — so rather than duplicate the vocabulary it does one bounded scan capped at 2000 rows, and when the cap is hit the page says the total is a FLOOR rather than lying.

WHAT IT REFUSES TO SHOW: file contents, ever. readSnapshot() returns { kind, path, bytes } and the type has nowhere to put a body; the diff compares byte counts. Prompts truncated in lists, full text only on the build's own page. The search term never enters an audit row, because searching here means searching customer prompts.

Hostile params all tested: ?status=constructor, ?family=toString, ?model=__proto__, ?vs=<another customer's build id>, and a SQL-injection attempt in the search box. No 500s, no prototype leakage.

WIRED BY ME afterwards: build_list.viewed and build.viewed added to the audit vocabulary (it was correctly using dashboard.viewed as a placeholder and flagging it), a first-class 'build' target type so build views do not pollute the per-site history filter, and a nav link.

IT ALSO CAUGHT A WRONG SCHEMA COMMENT: prisma/schema.prisma documented filesSnapshot as {path: content}. It is actually { source: {...}, build: {...} }. The agent believed the comment, reported "2 files", and only found the truth by querying live rows. Comment corrected.`],

  ["Diff view with approve / reject before applying", `Built — as a diff and an undo, NOT as approve/reject, and the panel says so in as many words.

THE HONEST VERDICT, which is the valuable part: approve/reject before applying is not achievable here. By the time anything is on screen the build has already written ProjectFile rows, charged credits, and the preview is showing the new site. There is no pending state to gate. So the controls are "Undo this build" and "Close", and the copy states plainly that undoing does not cancel the build and does not refund its credits. A real pre-apply gate would need the generate route to stage writes instead of committing them.

"Undo this build" is only offered when it is truthful — when the draft is actually at that build and a checkpoint sits directly behind it. Otherwise it is withheld with the reason.

Hand-rolled LCS line diff, no library, with two independent bounds so a Server Action payload cannot blow up.

PRUNED SNAPSHOTS get three DISTINCT honest outcomes rather than one generic error: the build's own snapshot cleared, the predecessor cleared (deliberately distinguished from a genuine first checkpoint, which reads differently), and a target pruned between listing and clicking.

One honesty bug it caught in its own work: after restore-then-reload the server only knows "draft differs from newest checkpoint", not which direction — the label said "back to the last build" when the last build was forwards. Now "restore the last build", true either way.`],

  ["Undo / redo across AI + manual edits", `Built alongside the diff view. Undo is defined over checkpoints, because a build snapshot is the only restorable state this product has.

The server answers exactly one question — does the draft still match the newest checkpoint? — by COMPARING THE TREES rather than guessing from timestamps. From there the cursor is maintained client-side.

Manual Brand and SEO saves now route through onManualSave so the cursor learns the draft drifted. Undo can put a manual edit back; nothing can redo it, because nothing captured it — so redo is cleared and says why, rather than offering a step that would silently skip the edit.` + AW],

  ["%Design-import: Figma / screenshot%", `PARTIALLY addressed by site import, and the remaining half is deliberate.

Reference-site import by URL is built: it extracts palette (up to 6 hexes), section rhythm from a CLOSED 12-word vocabulary, and corner radius. That is the entire list, and the type is the guarantee — ReferenceStyle has nowhere to put a sentence. A test asserts that none of a fixture page's headlines, testimonial, product name or class names survives.

The line drawn, and the reasoning: colour relationships and layout rhythm are not protectable and are what "I like that site" actually means. Copy, photographs and marks are — and reproducing them under a *.kodely.site domain we own makes their complaint our problem.

Figma import and screenshot-reference are NOT built. Screenshot needs the wizard's image path, which was out of scope.`],
];

const find = db.prepare(
  `SELECT c.id, c.title, c.columnId FROM cards c JOIN columns co ON co.id = c.columnId
   WHERE co.boardId = ? AND c.archivedAt IS NULL AND c.title LIKE ?`,
);
const addComment = db.prepare("INSERT INTO comments (id, cardId, authorName, body) VALUES (?, ?, 'Claude', ?)");
const maxPos = db.prepare("SELECT COALESCE(MAX(position), -1) AS p FROM cards WHERE columnId = ?");
const move = db.prepare("UPDATE cards SET columnId = ?, position = ? WHERE id = ?");

for (const [needle, note] of MOVES) {
  const card = find.get(B, needle.includes("%") ? needle : `%${needle}%`);
  if (!card) { console.log(`!! not found: ${needle}`); continue; }
  addComment.run(randomUUID(), card.id, note);
  if (card.columnId !== DONE) {
    move.run(DONE, maxPos.get(DONE).p + 1, card.id);
    console.log(`done | ${card.title.slice(0, 56)}`);
  } else {
    console.log(`cmnt | ${card.title.slice(0, 56)}`);
  }
}

// Two findings that are decisions for the owner, not work items.
const cards = [
  ["Kodely Pro: the credit thesis holds ~3x, but ONLY with prefix caching",
   `MEASURED, not asserted. A real client-only CRM foundation was built (lib/foundations/client-tracker, 25 files, 105KB) and compiled through the real buildSite in 1.5s.

The arithmetic, anchored on the one real measured build (13 files, 472 credits):
  foundation inlined, 8 turns, CACHED    ->  88 credits/build
  foundation inlined, 8 turns, UNCACHED  -> 363 credits/build
  model writes the kernel itself         -> 254 credits/build
  a MANIFEST instead of the full tree    -> 3.5 credits/build

So the thesis holds — but by roughly 3x, not 10x, and with two caveats that matter more than the headline:

1. UNCACHED, THE FOUNDATION COSTS MORE THAN NOT HAVING ONE. 363 vs 254. Prefix caching is the difference between saving 166 credits and losing 109. lib/agent.ts set cache_control on the system prompt only; the messages array — which carries the entire file tree via describeFiles() — had no breakpoint, so the largest repeated block in every request was re-sent at full price on all 8 turns. I HAVE NOW FIXED THAT, and it improves every build, not only Pro ones.

2. 88 credits is a tax on EVERY build including edits. Against a measured ~172-credit edit that is a ~50% surcharge on every follow-up, for re-sending a CSV exporter the model will not read.

THE REAL WIN IS THE MANIFEST: 1,213 tokens versus 30,221, i.e. 3.5 credits instead of 88. Every foundation file already carries purpose/exports strings and describeManifest() is implemented. Pair it with a read_file tool so the model pulls only what it needs.

It also chose the CRM over booking deliberately: a booking system's double-booking guard has to be a server-side constraint, so a client-only one would be exactly the pretends-to-work failure lib/agent.ts now forbids. A client tracker is genuinely complete for one person.`,
   "IDEA", "HIGH", ["pro", "cost", "decision"]],

  ["Site import ships OFF — it needs an egress-isolated worker to turn on",
   `The SSRF work is done and thorough, but the one control that matters most cannot be provided in application code, so the URL path fails closed behind KODELY_SITE_IMPORT_EGRESS. The paste path is always on and needs none of it.

The name is deliberate — it names the SERVICE, not the feature, so it is turned on by whoever stands that worker up rather than by whoever wants the feature.

What IS defended, each verified: https only (stricter than planned — a plaintext hop can be redirected by anyone on the path, and a typed http:// is upgraded while an http:// REDIRECT is refused); port 443 only; credentials in the URL refused; DNS resolved once and EVERY answer must classify public, so a name publishing one public and one private A record is refused entirely; IPv4 by denylist because public IPv4 is not contiguous, but IPv6 by ALLOWLIST inside 2000::/3 so ULAs and undelegated ranges are refused by construction; IPv4-mapped unwrapped, with a test asserting ::ffff:169.254.169.254 and ::ffff:a9fe:a9fe parse identically.

THE RE-BIND HOLE IS CLOSED BY PINNING, NOT RE-CHECKING. There is only ONE lookup: a pinned lookup function is handed to https.request and ignores the hostname, so no second resolution exists to disagree. Proven live — a request to a name that cannot resolve at all still landed on a local server, which could only happen via the pin.

31 hostile targets all refused in under 1ms each, with TCP tripwires on 127.0.0.1:443/80/8080/6379 that saw ZERO connections.

IF THE EGRESS WORKER IS NEVER BUILT, the agent's own recommendation is that the URL fields stay off permanently and paste plus screenshot carry the feature. That is a reasonable outcome, not a failure.`,
   "CHORE", "HIGH", ["security", "decision", "wizard"]],
];

const ins = db.prepare(
  "INSERT INTO cards (id, columnId, title, description, type, priority, labels, position) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)",
);
for (const [title, desc, type, prio, labels] of cards) {
  if (find.get(B, `%${title.slice(0, 30)}%`)) { console.log(`skip | ${title.slice(0, 50)}`); continue; }
  ins.run(randomUUID(), NA, title, desc, type, prio, JSON.stringify(labels), maxPos.get(NA).p + 1);
  console.log(`card | ${title.slice(0, 56)}`);
}

console.log(`\nDone: ${db.prepare("SELECT COUNT(*) n FROM cards WHERE columnId = ? AND archivedAt IS NULL").get(DONE).n}`);

