/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   SCROLLBAR — one Kodely-branded scrollbar, everywhere.
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Applied on :root rather than per-container, so every scrollable region in
   the product — the sidebar, a modal body, a code panel, a long settings
   page — gets it automatically with zero per-component work. A component
   that genuinely needs a different treatment (dark-on-dark inside the
   builder's code view, say) can still override `scrollbar-color` locally;
   this is the default, not a lock.

   Firefox uses the two-value `scrollbar-color` property directly — no way to
   restyle its shape, only its two colours, which is why the thumb colour
   below is chosen to read clearly at Firefox's fixed width too. Chrome,
   Safari and Edge use the `::-webkit-scrollbar*` pseudo-elements, which DO
   control shape — thinner, rounded, and with a hover state to match the
   rest of the product's hover language (border-mid on hover, same as a
   ghost button). Both engines are targeted; neither is left on its browser
   default, which is what made every scrollable panel look like it belonged
   to a different, older product. */
:root {
  scrollbar-width: thin;
  scrollbar-color: var(--border-strong) transparent;
}

::-webkit-scrollbar {
  width: 10px;
  height: 10px;
}
::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
  background: transparent;
}
::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
  background-color: var(--border-strong);
  border-radius: 999px;
  /* A transparent border matching the track colour insets the visible thumb
     within the scrollbar's own box — the standard trick for a slim rounded
     thumb that doesn't touch the edge of a rounded panel it scrolls inside. */
  border: 2px solid transparent;
  background-clip: padding-box;
}
::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb:hover {
  background-color: var(--brand-ring);
}
::-webkit-scrollbar-corner {
  background: transparent;
}

